Package: pgpid
Version: 0.1.0-1
Architecture: amd64
Maintainer: Sébastien Picardeau <piseb@mailo.com>
Installed-Size: 2584
Depends: gnupg, scdaemon, passwd, qrencode, zbar-tools, libgfshare-bin, librsvg2-bin, cups-client, cups-bsd, graphicsmagick, pandoc, texlive-extra-utils, libc6 (>= 2.38)
Recommends: sudo, imagemagick, bashlibs-common, bashlibs-convert
Suggests: facedetect, tesseract-ocr, gpg-wks-client
Section: utils
Priority: optional
Homepage: https://foopgp.org
Description: PGP certificates that say whose they are
 The PGP ID model gives a PGP certificate a decentralised entity
 identifier: who this key belongs to, said once and checked by people rather
 than by an authority.
 .
 pgpid reads and changes certificates through that model — identifiers,
 validity, ownertrust, addresses, vCard properties, avatars — vouches for
 other people, drives an OpenPGP security key, and puts a secret key on paper
 as QR codes so that any three of five fragments rebuild it.
 .
 What it depends on is what one of its actions spawns: gnupg for every one of
 them and scdaemon for the card half, qrencode and zbar-tools for the paper in
 both directions, libgfshare-bin for the sharing itself, pandoc and
 texlive-extra-utils to lay a sheet out, librsvg2-bin for the business card,
 the cups pair to ask which printers there are and to send to one,
 graphicsmagick to fit a picture into a certificate, and passwd for the
 local accounts.
 .
 Only three things are optional. Without sudo the system actions still run,
 but only for root, and there is then no SUDO_USER whose keyring and trust
 they can read — use their --from option to name one. Without imagemagick a
 scan reads PNG and JPEG but not PDF. Without the bash libraries the compiled
 tool is untouched; it is the two shell programs beside it that need them.
 .
 Those two are pgpid-gen, which generates a certificate from a passport, and
 pgpid-qrscan, which moves secrets onto a smartcard. Reading a passport is
 what facedetect and tesseract-ocr are for, and pgpid-gen asks gpg-wks-client
 whether an address supports WKD.
